'' A PARENTHESISED STRING CHAIN IS THE SAME CHAIN. FlattenConcat left '( "a" & w )' whole,
'' as ONE term that Check types STRING, so the wide or ustring term inside went unseen.
'' Measured against fbc, which prints aqbc, xUy and XUY:
''
''     print ( "a" & w ) & "bc"            tiko printed nothing
''     print "x" & ( u & "y" )             tiko printed "xU" and a NUL
''     print ucase( "x" & ( u & "y" ) )    ...and fb_PrintString over the UTF-16
''
'' Check's ConcatHasUString_ looks inside the parentheses too: fbc types that ucase USTRING.
'' 'print u & "a" & w' is a USTRING chain, and PRINT's wide branch took it and lost the "a".
'' '( x + 2 )' is still an ADD, and a user operator's '( a + b )' stays a call --
'' tests/mir/operator-string-result.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/concat-paren-subchain.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl void fb_PrintWstr(
'' EXPECT-OUT: fb_WstrAssignToA_Init(
'' EXPECT-OUT: fb_WstrLen(
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl void fb_PrintUStr(
'' EXPECT-OUT: fb_UStrUcase(
'' EXPECT-OUT: fb_WstrToUStr(
'' EXPECT-OUT: = add
'' EXPECT-OUT: fb_LongintToStr(
